+#### Proposing Wayfinder Goals and KPIs in a Draft
+
+A project-manager worker can also propose goals, KPI metrics, datapoints and KPIs. The Chief of Staff never writes: these tools are not available in chat mode, they are captured in the worker's draft, and Wayfinder is called only when the draft is approved and applied, exactly as for task proposals.
+
+| Tool | What it proposes | Applied as |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ta_propose_goal_create` | A goal (`name` required; `description`, `kind` slug defaulting to `milestone`, `parent_goal_id`, `target_date` as YYYY-MM-DD optional) | `POST /api/projects/<project>/goals` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_metric_create` | A KPI metric on a goal (`goal_id`, `label`, `unit`, `target` above 0, `cadence` of none, daily, weekly or monthly, `kind` of task_count or external; `aggregation` sum or latest, `require_gate`, `weight` optional) | `POST .../goals/<goal_id>/kpi-metrics` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_datapoint_add` | A number on an `external` metric (`metric_id`, `value`; `source` defaults to `manual`) | `POST .../kpi-metrics/<metric_id>/datapoints` |
+| `ta_propose_kpi_create` | An org-level KPI (`label`; `description`, `weight` optional; `org_wide` true for no project, default is this project) | `POST /api/orgs/<org>/kpis` |
+
+Every call needs `goal_run_id`. Numbers must be finite and within plus or minus 1e12, and text lengths are capped like Wayfinder's own limits. The project always comes from `[plan.wayfinder]` in `.ta/workflow.toml`, never from the proposal.
+
+**Building a hierarchy in one draft.** A goal or metric proposal returns a `ref` such as `ref:0`. Use it as `parent_goal_id` or `goal_id` (for a metric), or as `metric_id` (for a datapoint), in a later proposal of the same draft. The number is the proposal's position in the draft, counting from 0. Refs must point at an earlier proposal of the right kind. A datapoint against a proposed `task_count` metric is refused straight away, because Wayfinder derives that metric from tasks and refuses datapoints on it.
+
+**What a reviewer sees.** `ta draft view <draft-id>` lists each proposal with its fields spelled out (name, kind, parent, target date; label, unit, target, cadence, kind; value and source; KPI scope and weight), the `external_id` that will be sent, and the `ref` it can be referred to by.
+
+**What apply does.** Proposals replay in the order they were declared. Each create is sent with `external_id` set to `ta_draft:<draft-id>:<n>`, where `n` is the proposal's position in the draft, so a repeat never duplicates. Refs are replaced with the real ids Wayfinder returned, and those ids are recorded in `.ta/wayfinder-task-replay.log`. If a step fails, the steps that depend on it are blocked and the output names the failed step. Fix the cause and re-run `ta draft apply`: steps that already succeeded are skipped and their recorded ids still resolve refs.
+
+Errors say what to do. A 403 names the missing right: `tasks:write` for goals, metrics and datapoints, `kpis:manage` for org KPIs. A 409 is reported as a conflict, for example a datapoint sent to a `task_count` metric, or a different body already stored under the same `external_id`.
+
